 "limitations": "Other jobs shared the machine (load average between about 10 and 20 on 12 cores while this ran), so single figures can move by several percent; the Go and Rust jobs of a request alternate within a round so they see the same load. The earlier record's bundle was at the previous reference pin and ran on a less loaded machine, so compare the ratios of Go to Rust wall time and the step figures rather than absolute seconds. Go runs as the pinned exporter's sim command, the engine production runs, not the production image's own CLI. Process start is included for both engines, as in production.",
 "findings": [
  "Preparing a tank no longer dominates its job. The exporter read the target's swing from three reset simulations per stat aura combination and kept every one of them alive through the rolls' pointers into their attack tables. It now reads the rolls, and the rolls under a hardcast, from the simulation the stat auras effect builds for the combination, and the rolls hold copies. The Protection and Fury Protection Warriors' prepare step went from about 1.5 s and 610 MB to 0.28 s and 105 MB; the Paladin tanks and the Bear from about 0.25 s and 170 MB to 0.15 to 0.18 s and 101 to 103 MB. Every exported value, and every accepted fixture and Go golden, is byte identical.",
  "Routed Warrior tank jobs reach 0.88 and 0.90 in Go over Rust wall time, up from 0.36 and 0.39; the Paladin tanks and the Bear reach 1.02 to 1.06, up from 0.82 to 0.94.",
  "A whole routed job is now about as fast as a Go job, not clearly faster: median Go over Rust wall time 1.01 (1.00 without the tank builds), against 0.98 before, with 14 of the 27 requests faster in Rust. The Rust iteration loop is 1.27 times as fast as Go's, but a routed job still pays what a Go job does not: Python, about 0.05 s with its interpreter start, and the exporter's reset simulation for every stat aura combination, which the Go job never builds. Both jobs pay the exporter's process start of about 0.1 s.",
  "The gate no longer runs as its own process: sim --gate gates and simulates in one process, which removes a process start and a second parse of the prepared input.",
  "Four jobs at a time, Go ran 370.8 jobs a minute and routed Rust 349.2, against 390.3 and 315.7 before, with a peak resident set of 114.0 MB for one routed job against 656.3 MB.",
  "Every routed job gave the same DPS as the pinned Go engine with the same seed."
 ],
